   45: 
   46: 
   47: <p>This emulation mode attempts to emulate a Motorola MVME187 machine.
   48: Note that these were based on
   49: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motorola_88000">Motorola 88000</a>
   50: processors, not 68000.
   51: 
   52: 
   53: 
   54: 
   55: <p><br>
   56: <a name="openbsdmvme88kinstall"></a>
   57: <h3>OpenBSD/mvme88k:</h3>
   58: 
   59: <p>It is possible to run <a href="http://www.openbsd.org/mvme88k.html">OpenBSD/mvme88k</a>
   60: on an emulated Motorola MVME187 machine in the emulator.
   61: 
   62: <p>To install OpenBSD/mvme88k onto an emulated harddisk image, follow these
   63: instructions:
   64: 
   65: <p>
   66: <ol>
   67:   <li>Create an empty harddisk image, which will be the root disk
   68: 	that OpenBSD installs itself onto:<pre>
   69: 	<b>dd if=/dev/zero of=obsd_mvme88k.img bs=1024 count=1 seek=1900000</b>
   70: 
   71: </pre>
   72:   <li>Download the entire mvme88k directory from the ftp server:<pre>
   73: 	<b>wget -np -l 0 -r <a href="https://ftp.nluug.nl/OpenBSD/4.5/mvme88k/">https://ftp.nluug.nl/OpenBSD/4.5/mvme88k/</a></b>
   74: 
   75: </pre>
   76: 
   77:   <li>You now need to make an ISO image of the entire directory you downloaded.
   78: 	(I recommend using <tt>mkisofs</tt> for that purpose. If you don't
   79: 	already have <tt>mkisofs</tt> installed on your system, you need
   80: 	to install it in order to do this.)<pre>
   81: 	<b>mkisofs -o openbsd_mvme88k_4.5.iso -U ftp.nluug.nl/OpenBSD/</b>
   82: 
   83: </pre>
   84:   <li>Copy away the kernel, we'll need it later. But remove the rest of the
   85: 	downloaded tree.<pre>
   86: 	<b>cp ftp.nluug.nl/OpenBSD/4.5/mvme88k/bsd .
   87: 	rm -rf ftp.nluug.nl</b>
   88: </pre>
   89: 
   90:   <li>Start the emulator using this command line:<pre>
   91: 	<b>gxemul -e mvme187 -d obsd_mvme88k.img -d b:openbsd_mvme88k_4.5.iso -j 4.5/mvme88k/bsd.rd</b>
   92: 
   93: </pre>
   94: 	and proceed like you would do if you were installing OpenBSD
   95: 	on a real MVME187. There is a problem with finding the filesystem on
   96: 	the CDROM, since there is no disklabel [and/or because the emulator
   97: 	perhaps does not simulate CDROM TOCs well enough], but it's possible
   98: 	to mount
   99: 	the filesystem using manual intervention. Here is an example (from 4.4) of what
  100: 	an install can look like: (user input in <font color="#0000ff"><i>blue italic</i></font>)<pre>
  101: 	CPU0 is associated to 2 MC88200 CMMUs
  102: 	Copyright (c) 1982, 1986, 1989, 1991, 1993
  103: 		The Regents of the University of California.  All rights reserved.
  104: 	Copyright (c) 1995-2008 OpenBSD. All rights reserved.  http://www.OpenBSD.org
  105: 
  106: 	OpenBSD 4.4 (RAMDISK) #19: Sun Aug 10 21:03:44 GMT 2008
  107: 	    root@arzon.gentiane.org:/usr/src/sys/arch/mvme88k/compile/RAMDISK
  108: 	real mem = 67108864 (64MB)
  109: 	avail mem = 56791040 (54MB)
  110: 	mainbus0 at root: Motorola MVME187, 33MHz
  111: 	cpu0: M88100 rev 0x3, 2 CMMU
  112: 	cpu0: M88200 (16K) rev 0x9, full Icache, M88200 (16K) rev 0x9, full Dcache
  113: 	pcctwo0 at mainbus0 addr 0xfff00000: rev 0
