auditing.**  SEVERITY UNCHANGED IN KIND: the `else` arm is reachable, a plain `sb` at any offset ends the host, and the row stays HIGH.  Only the reachable-width claim is corrected.  *** AND MY ACCOUNT OF THE PANEL WAS WRONG IN A WAY THAT UNDER-CREDITED A SEAT.  Corrected 2026-08-31 by reading glm's answer instead of its verdict line. ***  I recorded that \"both packet-fed seats that engaged re-walked the figure and reconciled\", and that the split fell PRECISELY along the transport line.  **That is a cleaner story than the evidence supports.**  **glm -- PACKET-FED -- identified the assumption, derived the correct alternative numbers, and correctly refused to settle it:**  *\"the enumeration silently assumes misaligned guest accesses reach [the device]... widths 3/5/6/7 are guest-reachable only if this fork's MIPS core does not raise AdEL/AdES first.  **Under alignment enforcement the counts drop to 6144 declined / 3072 startFlag-set and the truncation chapter closes entirely** -- but the sb/sh doors and the draw-hazard survive untouched, so the defect stands either way.\"*  It also named my framing defect exactly: *\"'an ordinary 8-byte ld three bytes before the end' is an ld at a MISALIGNED address -- not ordinary on MIPS; its reachability is exactly the open premise.\"*  **6144 / 3072 is the number I computed only AFTER fable5 told me, and glm had it from the packet -- with both branches stated and the undecidable one flagged rather than guessed.  That is precisely what `panel_ollama.py`'s preamble asks a packet-fed seat to do, and it is the strongest packet-fed answer on record here.**  **THE HONEST TALLY:** codex, fable5 and grok (all FILE-READING) refuted the claim outright; **glm (PACKET-FED) identified it and bounded it**; minimax and deepseek (packet-fed) reconciled the wrong figure without flagging alignment.  *The doctrine's point survives and is even strengthened -- a packet-fed seat cannot SETTLE a repo fact -- but \"score a packet-fed agreement as zero\" is too blunt: glm did not agree, it CONDITIONED.  A conditioned answer from a seat that cannot check is worth more than an unconditioned one from a seat that can.*  *** THE 25-ROW KILL TABLE, NINE BUILDS, AND THE OBVIOUS FIX IS NOT THE RIGHT ONE. ***  `mR` (latched decline + `goto` to the common exit) **25/25**.  `mB` (early `return 1`) also 25/25 but not recommended -- it duplicates nothing today only because the tail's remaining step is a no-op, and diverges silently the day the tail grows.  **`fix` -- the shape the brief's own logic recommends, decline + `startFlag = 0` + fall through -- scores 24/25 and the red row is the point:** a declined 1-byte write still reaches the switch, which prints `[ sgi_de: TODO: non-zero CRIME_DE_SCISSOR: ... ]` about **a write that never happened**.  Product code announcing a TODO for a discarded `idata` -- the same wrong-record-in-a-diagnostic class the ACCEPT member was discovered through.  **`goto <label>` to a common exit is HOUSE IDIOM, not an invention** -- 10 device files use it, including `dev_sgi_mec.c` in this same SGI family.  The grep is the answer to \"is this too clever\".  **ONE latch bit, and it must be a NEW one.**  Per-message-site gives one bit here (one guard, one text).  But `mC` -- reusing #497's `SGIRE_W_TLBA_LEN` -- scores 23/25: the four handlers share one malloc'd struct, so any odd-width access to `sgi_re`'s TLB_A window silences `sgi_de`'s decline entirely.  `1<<6` is free.  **ACCEPT ROWS AT `+0x7fc` AND `+0x7f8`, NOT THE NOMINAL LAST SLOTS.**  `+0xffc`/`+0xff8` cannot carry an accept row because they trip `sgideprimop` -- the last legal slots WITH startFlag CLEAR are the real anchors.  *That is a fact about the device, not a convenience, and it belongs in the row comments.*  Plus a FIFTH row `tail_acc`: an accepted width at `+0xfd0` must STILL reach `draw_primitive`, and it is the only row that kills a mutant clearing startFlag unconditionally -- without it, disabling all drawing sweeps clean.  **THE SMALLEST BREAKING EDIT IS BLIND TO A LOWER-HALF-ONLY TABLE.**  Delete the `goto` and keep the latch: 19/25 against this table, but **25/25 against any table whose rows all sit below 0x800**, because no such offset can reach `draw_primitive`.  *That is the anchor rule one level up -- rows that never vary the START bit cannot see a repair that only matters when it is set.*  *THE GOTO-IDIOM COUNT, DERIVED RATHER THAN QUOTED, because the seat's figure and my first grep disagreed: `grep -rl '[^a-z_]goto ' src/devices/*.c` gives **10 files**, matching the seat.  My first attempt gave 6 because it pinned three specific label names (`do_return`, `ret`, `out`) and `dev_sgi_mec.c` uses `goto skip`.  **The figure that actually bears on this fix is narrower and better: 8 device files use `goto` INSIDE a `DEVICE_ACCESS` body** -- dc7085, i80321, le, mk48txx, ne2000, pvr, vr41xx, wdc -- walked by scanning each handler's braces rather than the whole file.  Either way the idiom is in-tree and the question \"is a goto too clever here\" is answered by the tree.*  *** #498 SHIPPED.  Fix + 25-row detector, and the detector's FIRST DRAFT SHIPPED A CLAIM IT COULD NOT BACK. ***  I wrote 22 rows and a contract naming `tail_acc` as *the only row that catches a repair hoisted to every access* -- **while `tail_acc` was not in the file.**  Three of the seat's measured rows were missing (`hi_kill`, `tail_acc`, `tail_acc_x`), and without them the 22/25 mutant that silently DISABLES ALL DRAWING would have swept clean.  That is the CLAIM -> ROW pre-table check failing inside a detector's own header, caught by diffing my row set against the measured one rather than by trusting my own contract.  Now 25/25, matching the seat's set exactly; the gate's row list and both denominators repinned 22 -> 25.  *The 22-row artifact was DELETED rather than kept beside the 25-row one: it measured an incomplete instrument, and a preserved log whose instrument was wrong is not evidence, it is a trap for whoever cites it.*  **`tail_acc_x` pins a host DEATH on purpose** -- the accepted-width access reaches `draw_primitive` and dies there, which is `sgideprimop`.  When that row is fixed this one goes RED BY SUCCEEDING, so it inherits the oracle-correction ordering: re-pin first, measure the committed build red, then edit.  *** PASS 2 FOUND A REAL DETECTOR HOLE, TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY, AND IT IS NOW CLOSED. ***  `len == 2` was not pinned as a live decline anywhere.  The witness killed on sb, sh, lbu AND lhu; the detector's survival and answer rows used sb and lbu ONLY, and `sh` appeared solely as the SECOND access in `two_widths_*`, whose oracle is *\"len=2 is never announced\"*.  **That row stays GREEN whether len==2 is ACCEPTED, still KILLED with the old text, or correctly DECLINED -- three machines behind one green row -- and the ONE latch bit is why: after `sb`, a later `sh` is SUPPOSED to be silent, so silence proves nothing.**  fable5 predicted the escaping edit as `else if (len == 4 || len == 2)` at a full 25/25 and walked it row by row rather than building it during a live panel; grok reached the same hole from the witness side.  **Four rows added (`lo_kill2`, `lo_decl2`, `lo_alive2`, `lo_sent2`), each in its OWN session so the fresh latch makes the len=2 announcement observable at all.  Detector 25 -> 29, all green, gate repinned.**  *** AND THE PROCESS BREACH, RECORDED RATHER THAN BACK-WRITTEN: #498 IS THE FIRST POST-MANDATE KILL-TABLE ROUND WITH NO `PRETABLE.out`. ***  Five sibling rounds carry one (gdromcondlies, gdromexitsix, wdcaddtoinbufbound, wdcaltoffset, widthkillclass -- this round's own parent).  **Both of its costs are visible in this round**: the CLAIM -> ROW check is exactly what polices *a contract naming `tail_acc` while `tail_acc` is not in the file*, which I caught late and by diffing; and the de-escalation enumeration -- every predicate that gated death, accept side AND refusal side per reachable width -- is where the len==2 hole would have surfaced at WRITING time.  *Back-writing the block now would violate its own mtime-order rule, so the honest record is \"skipped, costs enumerated\".*  **AND ONE DISPOSITION WAS WRONG: deleting the 22-row artifact.**  The house remedy for a misleading record is FREEZE-WITH-BANNER, not destruction -- REVIEW_FINDINGS.md's scope banner and the seat table \"kept for its REASONING only\" are the precedents.  The deletion was pre-commit and is therefore unrecoverable, and it strips the evidence from three durable prose claims that cite it.  A `WRONG-INSTRUMENT` banner would have cost three lines.  *** PASS 2's MEASURING SEAT FOUND TWO MORE 25/25 ESCAPES THAT SURVIVED THE 29-ROW VERSION, BOTH FLOOR BREACHES, BOTH ONE KEYSTROKE.  L2 -- repaired in the follow-up commit. ***  **`bracegoto`: move `goto de_declined;` INSIDE the latch braces.**  Only the FIRST decline then jumps; every later one falls through to the register switch AND `draw_primitive()` -- measured `exit1`, *the exact host death this round exists to remove*.  It escaped because **every row in the detector exercised the FIRST decline of a fresh session**; `lo_latch` came closest with two declines but chose two lower-half offsets, both landing on the switch's silent `default:`.  **`bit5collide`: `1 << 6` -> `1 << 5`, one character**, colliding with `SGIRE_W_LINB_LEN` and silencing the decline outright.  `sharedlatch` tripped only TLB_A, making it a **POINT TEST AGAINST A SIX-MEMBER SET** -- `grep -c 'd->warned |=' ` gives 7 sites, and five of the six partners escaped.  **Both repairs are BOUNDED -- no new rows and no new sessions**, which is why they are a repair rather than the `#392` chain: the upper-half sessions gain one lower-half decline in front, and `sharedlatch` trips all six siblings.  29/29 after.  **AND A ROW OF MINE WAS VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION.**  I moved `hi_kill`'s oracle off the measured form onto `len == 4` -- an ACCEPTED width on every build in the story -- so the channel could not vary.  Measured identical on the pre-fix build, on all eight of this round's mutants, post-fix, and on all five of the seat's; even a verbatim reinstatement left it green.  Re-pinned onto the measured len=1 upper-half form.  *** THE PRETABLE SKIP IS NO LONGER AN ABSTRACT PROCESS POINT: `#490`'s OWN PRETABLE BLOCK PRESCRIBED \"a latch-bit collision and a brace slip\", AND THOSE ARE MY TWO ESCAPES BY NAME. ***  Its GUARD-FIELD check asks *who else writes this field, and can they set my bit?* -- seven sites here.  Skipping the block cost exactly the coverage it exists to find, and that is measured rather than argued.  **FLOOR (a) AND (b) DO HOLD**, measured because no artifact showed it: a verbatim reinstatement of the pre-fix arm scores **14/25**, eleven rows red.  *** AND I BLINDED THREE SEATS: THE PASS-2 BRIEF TRUNCATED THE DIFF AT 7000 CHARACTERS AND THE 1545 BYTES CUT WERE THE ENTIRE FIX -- the latch, the message, the goto and the label. ***  The brief was 13.5 KB against a 30 KB cap, so the truncation bought nothing; the cap was arbitrary and I never checked what it removed.  **Two seats refused to answer around it and named the exact cut point**, which is the honest packet-fed response.  All three packet-fed seats were RE-FIRED against the complete diff rather than letting the record show six answers where three were blind.  *** AND THE `mC` 23/25 FIGURE IS CONFOUNDED -- codex checked the generator rather than the score. ***  It combined the shared latch bit with the `startFlag = 0` fallthrough, so its two reds (`dec_switch`, `sharedlatch`) come from TWO edits.  Pure bit-0 reuse against `mR` would lose only the latch row.  **The conclusion is unchanged and the citation is wrong**: the own-bit requirement is established by `bit5collide` -- a genuine one-character edit escaping all 25 rows -- not by 23/25.  *A score cited for a property its mutant did not isolate is the frozen-number class wearing a different hat: the number is real, the thing it is offered as evidence for is not what was measured.*",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:34961:   "id": "mk48nvramkill",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:34965:   "title": "dev_mk48txx.c: EIGHT offsets inside the device's own mapped range (0x1ff0-0x1ff7) fall to a default: arm that calls exit(1), so an ordinary guest read of the MK48T08 clock page ends the emulator process -- and machine_mvme88k.c attaches the device unconditionally",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:34966:   "note": "FILED 2026-09-10.  Surfaced by the GPT-6 Astra exit audit (regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_exitaudit_20260910.txt), which ranked it I1/E1 -- its highest impact and lowest effort.  EVERY ELEMENT BELOW WAS RE-DERIVED LOCALLY by opening the files, not taken from the seat.\n\n*** THE ARITHMETIC, enumerated rather than reasoned. ***  The device is registered for MK48TXX_LEN = MK48T08_CLKSZ = 8192 bytes, so relative_addr spans [0, 0x2000).  The early accept block passes everything OUTSIDE [CLKOFF, CLKOFF+ISEC) straight through to the RAM path with a `goto ret'.  With MK48T08_CLKOFF = 0x1ff0 and MK48TXX_ISEC = 9, the switch is therefore reached for exactly the nine offsets 0x1ff0..0x1ff8 -- and the switch has exactly ONE case, MK48T08_CLKOFF + MK48TXX_ICSR = 0x1ff8.  **The other EIGHT, 0x1ff0 through 0x1ff7, fall to `default:', print an `unimplemented' diagnostic and call exit(1).**\n\n*** REACHABILITY IS NOT THEORETICAL. ***  machine_mvme88k.c attaches it unconditionally -- `mk48txx addr=0x%x', 0xfffc0000 -- in the same block that attaches the PCC2 SCSI this project has been running rounds against all week, so the machine is live and exercised. The eight offsets are ORDINARY BYTES of the chip's clock/NVRAM page: nothing in the guest has to do anything unusual to touch them, and firmware that checksums or dumps the NVRAM sweeps the whole 8 KB and walks straight into them.  There is no guard, no validation and no way for a guest to discover the region is fatal before touching it.\n\n*** AND THE BOUNDARY LOOKS DELIBERATE, WHICH IS WHY IT SURVIVED. ***  The accept block's comment says `Reads and writes to the RAM part of the mk48txx, or the clock registers, are OK', and the test READS as though it means `everything except the clock registers'.  But the upper bound is CLKOFF + ISEC, i.e. the SECONDS register -- so the block hands the real time-of-day registers (ISEC..IYEAR, 0x1ff9..0x1fff) to the RAM path, which is harmless because mk48txx_update_regs() writes into the same d->reg[] array, and it captures 0x1ff0..0x1ff7 into the fatal switch, which is not.  **The bound is one constant away from correct in the direction nobody checks.**\n\nFIX SHAPE, not yet adjudicated -- no panel has seen this: the option set's member 3 (DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE) or member 6 (BACKED ACCEPT) both fit, and BACKED ACCEPT is probably right because d->reg[] ALREADY has storage for those offsets -- the array is MK48TXX_LEN bytes, so the bytes exist and the handler simply refuses to use them.  That makes this closer to a boundary correction than to a new policy.  A one-constant change to the accept block would do it; the round owes the check of WHICH constant is right against mk48txxreg.h rather than assuming.\n\nWITNESS: none built.  The vehicle looks cheap -- mvme88k is an in-tree machine and a debugger-driven byte read at 0xfffc1ff0 is a rung-3 probe -- but a witness has NOT been constructed and this row does not claim one.  The rung-3 ATTEMPT is owed before it can be recorded documented-only.\n\nRELATED: this is NOT a member of [[shiftubcensus]] or [[exitsweep]]'s repaired set; it is one of the 76 unowned runtime error-exit sites the exit audit enumerated across 24 files, and it is the one that ranked first on both axes."
